\subsection{Attempt: a task that requires information exchange}
The source distinguishes interaction from collaboration. I tested a concrete way to make group output depend on distributed information: each of $n\ge1$ participants holds a private input bit, and every participant must output the parity of all inputs. Communication is allowed only across edges of a fixed undirected graph. No participant initially knows another's bit, and deterministic protocols must work for every input.
\begin{proposition}
This task is solvable exactly when the communication graph is connected. If connected, at most $2(n-1)$ one-bit transmissions suffice.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If the graph is disconnected, choose a participant in one component and flip a bit in another. No message can cross between the components. Inducting over communication steps, every local state and received message in the first component is unchanged by this flip. Its participant therefore produces the same output on two inputs whose global parities differ, so the protocol fails on one.

If connected, select a spanning tree and root it. Each leaf sends its bit toward the root. Each internal vertex waits for its children's messages, takes their parity together with its own bit, and sends that one bit to its parent. Induction from the leaves shows each transmitted bit is the parity of the corresponding subtree. The root obtains the global parity after $n-1$ upward transmissions and broadcasts it down the same tree in $n-1$ more transmissions. Every participant then has the required answer. For $n=1$, no transmission is needed.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
For a four-participant path, the protocol uses at most six bits; enumerating its 16 input vectors verifies all returned parities. This is an information-dependence guarantee, not proof that the participants discuss meaning, learn equally, create original mathematics or collaborate authentically. The precise failed inference would be to equate a communication-feasible task with a validated collaboration intervention. Instructor collaboration and student content creation also require separate specifications absent from this model.

